<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Wright (governor)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1716">1716</a>–<a href="/years/1785">1785</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Sir James Wright, 1st Baronet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Wright was the last royal governor of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, serving from 1760. Under him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> plantations worked by enslaved Africans spread along the coast, and Georgia's frontier was extended by land cessions from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a>. Patriots drove him out in 1776, but he returned in 1779 after British forces reoccupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a> and governed there until the British evacuation in 1782.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-wright-governor.jpg" alt="James Wright (governor)" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrea Soldi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Wright_(governor).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James Wright was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 8 May 1716, a grandson of Sir Robert Wright, Lord Chief Justice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In 1730 his father, Robert Wright, went with Robert Johnson to the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> and served as its Chief Justice until 1739. James followed and practised law in Charleston, entered Gray's Inn in London on 14 August 1741 and in 1747 was named the colony's attorney-general, while building up plantation lands. In 1757 he returned to London as agent for South Carolina.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In May 1760 he was named Lieutenant Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> under Henry Ellis, and on Ellis's resignation became governor in November 1760. He moved his property and business from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> to Georgia, and with peace for a time with the French and Spanish he negotiated with the Indians and the Crown to open new lands. Despite the Sons of Liberty, Georgia was the only colony to import and use the revenue stamps of the Stamp Act of 1765. In 1768 he founded Wrightsboro, a settlement of 12,000 acres for displaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> from North Carolina. Georgia sent no delegates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a> in 1774.</p><p>In 1775 he dismissed the assembly, but a revolutionary congress met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a> that summer and chose delegates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-continental-congress">Second Continental Congress</a>. Early in 1776 rebels entered his home and briefly held him; on 11 February he escaped with the help of John Mullryne and Josiah Tattnall to HMS Scarborough. After a failed attempt to retake Savannah by sea he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Back in Savannah once British troops had retaken it, he restored colonial rule over large parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, the only colony the British regained after being expelled, and defended the city against American and French attempts to capture it. He withdrew on 11 July 1782.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His large properties were seized by the revolutionary governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. Wright retired to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 20 November 1785 and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>. With his wife Sarah Maidman he had nine children, among them Sir James Wright, 2nd Baronet. Wright Square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a> and the town of Wrightsboro are named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Wright (governor)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Trustees surrendered the charter, and Georgia became a royal province in 1752, with rice plantations spreading along the coast under governor <b>James Wright</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-georgia">Province of Georgia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1760" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James Wright (governor)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1760">What was here in 1760? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
